  "rationale": "Baseline 0.9672; only summary_quality (0.934) imperfect; 4 sub-criteria (accuracy, completeness, faithfulness, conciseness). R4 added a brevity nudge to section_summary.md (+0.0016 KEPT) \u2014 the only content lever that empirically moved. R1 (-0.106) and R6 (-0.105) each pushed length/coverage/no-add and 32k-truncated 2 rows. The two skills that describe summary length are now INCONSISTENT: section_summary.md says 'prefer 1 or 2 sentences' but output_schema.md's inline schema sketch still shows '(1-3 sentences)'. Since the schema sketch is the model's output template, it anchors generation and partially cancels R4's prose-level brevity signal. This edit aligns the schema sketch's parenthetical with R4's exact brevity language: '(1-2 sentences; a 3rd only if a numeric threshold, exception, or cross-reference would otherwise be omitted)'. No key, field name, type, order, or structural element changes \u2014 schema_adherence and output_json_valid stay 1.0. Materially different from R1/R6: strictly length-REDUCING (both reverts were triggered by content/length additions pushing 2 rows past max_tokens); the edit only tightens an already-allowed sentence budget. Materially different from R4: different file (output_schema.md vs section_summary.md), different surface (template vs prose). No clash: identity.md 'faithful short summary' (aligned); section_summary.md identical brevity (reinforced, not contradicted); hierarchy/citations/exclusions structural. Predicted \u0394: +0.000 to +0.005 on summary_quality via 1-2 examples flipping conciseness 4->5 when the schema-anchored writer previously defaulted to the full 3-sentence budget. Downside bounded: strictly shorter output cannot truncate; worst case is a true noop if R4 already captured the full conciseness signal.",
